bin/

# Provisioning & Secret State Files
vars.sh
**/vars.sh
vars.sh.tmp
**/vars.sh.tmp
*.vars.sh

# Environment Files
# install.env is the hand-authored install configuration and holds credentials.
# It needs naming outright: the patterns below anchor on a leading dot, so
# neither `.env` nor `.env.*` matches it. Same for tags.env, which is committed.
install.env
**/install.env
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!*.env.example
k8s-operator/cover.out

# Python artifacts
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.pytest_cache/
.venv/

# Coverage measurement (`make coverage`)
.coverage-data/
coverage.xml
coverage-go.xml

# Terraform working directories. Lock files are ignored because this repo
# ships modules and a copy-paste example, not a deployed root configuration —
# consumers should commit their own multi-platform lock in their own repo.
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl

# Terraform state and variable files hold credentials and infrastructure
# details — never commit them. Committed examples keep the .example suffix.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example
terraform.tfvars.json

# Terraform override files are environment-local by convention: lifecycle.sh
# writes (and normally removes) providers_lifecycle_override.tf mid-import,
# and a user's backend override belongs to their environment, not the example.
*_override.tf

# Local build & release packaging output
build/
*.tgz

# Go build output left in a working directory. A build without -o writes the
# executable next to wherever it was run, and that lands in one of two places:
# the module root, from `go build ./cmd/<name>` or from `go build cmd/main.go`
# (k8s-operator's Makefile passes -o bin/manager, so only dropping the flag
# produces k8s-operator/main); or inside the package, from the in-place form
# k8s-operator/cmd/k8s-event-watcher/README.md documents. Either leaves tens of
# megabytes for `git add -A` to offer, so both locations are listed for every
# package that can reach them.
#
# Full paths rather than bare names. A pattern with no slash matches at every
# depth, so a bare `a2a` would ignore the a2a/ module itself and a bare
# `k8s-event-watcher` its source directory; the slash anchors each pattern to
# this file's directory, the repo root.
#
# Two things deliberately absent. `go build ./...` needs no rule at all: given
# more than one package it discards the binaries, which is why `make verify`
# uses it. And a2a/authcallout, gateway, hermes-bridge and worker-adapter have
# no module-root entry because a same-named directory already occupies a2a/ and
# go refuses to overwrite it -- only their in-package copies are reachable.
k8s-operator/main
k8s-operator/k8s-event-watcher
k8s-operator/cmd/k8s-event-watcher/k8s-event-watcher
a2a/a2a
a2a/cmd/a2a/a2a
a2a/cmd/authcallout/authcallout
a2a/cmd/gateway/gateway
a2a/cmd/hermes-bridge/hermes-bridge
a2a/cmd/worker-adapter/worker-adapter

# drift-detector's sources arrive with #1594. The rules precede them on purpose:
# they match nothing until that merges, and the binary is never offered after.
k8s-operator/drift-detector
k8s-operator/cmd/drift-detector/drift-detector

# Per-developer Claude Code state (session settings). The shared
# slash commands in .claude/commands/ and the review skills in .agents/skills/
# are committed; .claude/settings.json and .claude/settings.local.json stay
# personal. settings.json is deliberately *not* committed even though the
# live-test lease hooks want to live there -- a settings.json on a branch runs
# without being asked, and .claude/commands/pr-review-batch.md checks out fork
# branches. .claude/settings.json.example is the copy you opt into; see
# docs/designs/live-test-lease.md. `.claude/*` rather than `.claude/` so git
# descends into the directory: a rule that excludes the directory itself can
# never be undone by a nested .gitignore or a negation, because git does not
# look inside it.
#
# .claude/skills and .claude/rules are symlinks into .agents/, the tool-neutral
# home Gemini CLI and Codex already read; Claude Code scans only .claude/. No
# trailing slash on those two negations: git records a symlink as a file.
.claude/*
!.claude/commands/
!.claude/settings.json.example
!.claude/skills
!.claude/rules
